<BaseLayout
  title="About | Employable PM"
  description="Amir Baruch on AI workflows, coding agents, and staying employable the hands-on way."
>
  <p class="kicker"><span class="accent">~/about</span></p>
  <h1>Amir Baruch</h1>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      I've spent my career around things that move.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Bolt, SAP, TomTom, Telenav, …now Stellantis.</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
      Somewhere between the code and the roadmap I found the spot I like
      best: close enough to the tech to be dangerous, close enough to the
      customer to know what matters.
    </p>
    <p>
      This blog is my notebook. I write about AI workflows, coding agents,
      and building real things. The thesis is simple: PMs should be
      technical enough that nobody can bullshit them.
    </p>
    <p>
      The contrarian bit: everyone says PMs should stay out of the code.
      I think that's backwards. My best product calls came from
      understanding what the machine can actually do.
    </p>
    <p>
      Day job: Director of Product Management (Geo, Maps and Agentic AI)
      at Stellantis. Mapping platforms across 12 automotive brands,
      leading a team of PMs.
    </p>
    <p>
      Here: learning in public. Sometimes wrong in public too.
    </p>
